# XSH setbuf()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.setbuf.001
anchor     functions/setbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setvbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With setbuf(f, buf) on a new stream opened "w", where buf is the
           start of an array of BUFSIZ + 64 bytes filled with '#', after 2 *
           BUFSIZ + 100 bytes are written with fputc() and the stream is
           closed, the file holds those bytes and the last 64 bytes of the
           array are still '#'.
note       Equivalent to setvbuf(f, buf, _IOFBF, BUFSIZ), whose size argument
           is the size of the array; the contents of the first BUFSIZ bytes
           are unspecified at any time, so only the bytes past them are
           looked at.
test       t/001-own-buffer.c

assertion  XSH.setbuf.002
anchor     functions/setbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setvbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      1988
says       On a new stream opened "w" after setbuf(f, NULL), once fputc('a')
           has returned (no fflush()), the file holds "a".
note       disputed (Q-33): setvbuf() says _IONBF shall cause unbuffered
           output, but XSH 2.5 and ISO C make support for the buffering
           characteristics implementation-defined; a FAIL is logged as
           INSPECT.
test       t/002-null-unbuffered.c

assertion  XSH.setbuf.003
anchor     functions/setbuf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       On a new stream opened "r" on a file holding "0123", after
           setbuf(f, buf) with a BUFSIZ-byte array, and likewise after
           setbuf(f, NULL), successive getc() calls return '0', '1', '2', '3'
           and then EOF.
test       t/003-reading.c
